## A landscape lit by the baroque

A century before Marienbad was even born, western Bohemia lived through its golden age: monasteries grew rich, pilgrimage sites flourished, and a genius named Jan Blažej Santini-Aichel invented an architecture the world had never seen. Five baroque stops, each an easy half-day trip — and each unforgettable.

## 1. Kladruby Monastery — Santini's cathedral of light

**By car**: 40 minutes | **Time on site**: 1.5–2.5 hours

When the Benedictines needed to renew their medieval church, Santini built them something scarcely believable: a church that looks Gothic yet breathes baroque. The vaults twist into stars, the dome above the crossing recalls a papal tiara, and the light inside changes colour with the time of day. One of the largest churches in Bohemia and the summit of the style known as Baroque Gothic.

## 2. Mariánská Týnice — a dome completed after three centuries

**By car**: 55 minutes | **Time on site**: 1–2.5 hours

Santini's pilgrimage church of the Annunciation had bad luck with history: left unfinished, it decayed to the brink of collapse. But the story has a happy ending — the eastern cloister with its dome was completed only recently, following Santini's original plans, more than three hundred years on. The radiant white complex now houses the Museum of the Northern Plzeň Region and ranks among the country's most beautiful baroque spaces.

## 3. Manětín — a town that is a sculpture gallery

**By car**: 50 minutes | **Time on site**: 1.5–3 hours

Manětín is called "the baroque pearl of western Bohemia", and rightly so: around the chateau and church stand more than thirty open-air sandstone statues of saints, angels and allegories, the work of the local 18th-century sculpture workshop. The chateau offers guided tours, but Manětín's magic works on a simple stroll: here the baroque is not behind glass — it stands right in the street.

## 4. Chlum Svaté Maří — a pilgrimage site above the Ohře valley

**By car**: 40 minutes | **Time on site**: 1–1.5 hours

For seven hundred years pilgrims streamed here to a miraculous statue of the Virgin — and the baroque complex of the Knights of the Cross with the Red Star that rose above the Ohře valley is visible for miles. The twin-towered façade, frescoed interior and chapel-lined cloisters form one of the most impressive sacred ensembles in western Bohemia. And you will almost always have it to yourself.

## 5. The Kappl near Waldsassen — a church built on the number three

**By car**: 30 minutes | **Time on site**: 30–60 minutes

Just across the border, on a rise above Waldsassen, stands a church without equal in Europe: Georg Dientzenhofer built the pilgrimage church of the Holy Trinity entirely on the number three — three towers, three apses, three altars, a circular plan of three interlocking parts. From outside it looks like a fairy-tale castle; inside, the perfect geometry of faith surrounds you. Stop at Waldsassen's famous abbey library on the way.

## The baroque circuit

All five places combine neatly into two full-day loops: Kladruby with Manětín and Mariánská Týnice to the east, Chlum and the Kappl to the west. The complete overview, castles and monasteries included, is in our [day-trips catalogue](/en/day-trips).

